<PageLayout
  title="Affiliate Disclosure"
  description="How Moshakha makes money, which links are affiliate links, and how that does and does not affect what we recommend."
  canonical="/affiliate-disclosure"
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <p>
    Moshakha is a reader-supported publication. This page explains exactly how we make
    money, so you can judge our recommendations with that in mind.
  </p>

  <h2>Amazon Associates</h2>
  <p>
    Moshakha is a participant in the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program, an affiliate
    advertising program designed to provide a means for sites to earn advertising fees by
    advertising and linking to Amazon.com. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying
    purchases.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you click a link on this site that goes to Amazon and you buy something, Amazon
    may pay us a small percentage of that sale. <strong>The price you pay is exactly the
    same</strong> — the commission comes out of Amazon's margin, not your pocket.
  </p>

  <h2>How to spot an affiliate link</h2>
  <p>
    Every affiliate link on this site is marked up with <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code>,
    and any page containing affiliate links carries a visible disclosure near the top. Buy
    buttons that lead to a retailer are affiliate links. Links to manufacturer documentation,
    standards bodies, or other editorial sources are not.
  </p>

  <h2>What commission does not buy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No brand pays for placement.</strong> We do not sell positions in our rankings, and we never have.</li>
    <li><strong>No sponsored posts.</strong> We do not publish brand-written or brand-approved content.</li>
    <li><strong>Commission rate does not affect ranking.</strong> Some categories pay better than others; we do not steer recommendations toward them.</li>
    <li><strong>We will tell you not to buy.</strong> Where a cheaper product wins, or where the honest answer is "you don't need this", we say so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Our testing and research standards</h2>
  <p>
    Our recommendations are based on hands-on use where we have it, and on structured
    research — manufacturer specifications, material and construction analysis, and
    aggregated long-term owner feedback — where we do not. We tell you which is which on
    each page rather than implying lab testing we haven't done.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If anything here is unclear, or you think we've got a recommendation wrong, please
    <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>. Corrections are noted on the article itself.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
